topology forge (formerly TopoForge) NEWS -- user-visible changes

Copyright 2026 Bryant Frankford <nathanielfrankford@gmail.com> and
Corwin Brust <corwin@bru.st> and Contributors
SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-SA-4.0

Version 1.14 (2026-11-04; checked: tests/run.sh, the project-files check, the
browser kit and the embedding kit green, with their planted faults; see
RESULT.txt and RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release packed or signed
yet)

- A page's own Shift can show the peek (Corwin, 2026-11-04, 04:36: "Please do
  work the change to toplogy forge for Shift and the text cut off on later
  pages").  The app hears its own keys only while the focus is in it, so on a
  page whose frames had the focus the peek waited for the pointer to move.
  With peek on, TopoForge.peekKey(true) when the page hears Shift go down shows
  the card of the shape under the pointer's last place on the drawing (or,
  with the pointer off it, of the one selected shape), and peekKey(false) when
  Shift goes up puts back what the map showed.  It returns what shows ({ id,
  name } or null), does nothing with peek off, and takes no event.
- A big card whose title is too long to fit at 1.10's sizes even alone (a
  match's card whose first line is a long sentence, on a later page) was cut
  off by its box ("... for people and their agents:").  Such a title is now
  shortened to the words that fit, at a word's end, with an ellipsis; its
  whole text stays in a tooltip, in cardPage(), in what the live region says
  and in topoforge:preview's cards.  A title that fits is drawn as before.

Version 1.13 (2026-11-03; checked: tests/run.sh, the project-files check, the
browser kit and the embedding kit green, with their planted faults; see
RESULT.txt and RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release packed or signed
yet)

- A peek at a placed shape's card, for a page that asks (switch peek; it turns
  preview on; Corwin, 2026-11-03: "when hovering a shape on the main stage
  area it would be good to have a modifier key that shows the
  flash-card/trading-card like we get with no modifier").  Hold Shift with the
  pointer over a shape on the drawing and the overview map shows that shape's
  card, the same card preview([id]) shows (so the big card fills the map when
  bigcard is on); move to another shape and the card follows.  Let Shift go,
  or leave the shape or the drawing, and the map shows again exactly what it
  showed before: the page's own preview, on the card it was on, or nothing.
  By keyboard: with the drawing focused and one shape selected, press Shift on
  its own.  The modifier is Shift because a lone Alt takes the focus to the
  browser's menu on Windows, and Ctrl zooms with the wheel.  The peek only
  listens: Shift-click, Shift-drag, Shift+wheel, Shift on a resize handle and
  Shift with the arrow keys do what they did.  The page is told
  (topoforge:peek: the shape's id and name, then id null); with the map
  hidden, nothing is drawn but the page is still told.
- TopoForge.fitScale(): the zoom Fit to window would set now, read without
  moving the view, so a page can put "fit" in the middle of its own zoom
  slider.  Fit to window and fitScale() share one calculation; Fit sets
  exactly the view it set in 1.12.

Version 1.12 (2026-11-03; checked: tests/run.sh, the project-files check, the
browser kit and the embedding kit green, with their planted faults; see
RESULT.txt and RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release packed or signed
yet)

- The card that fills the map, for a page that asks (switch bigcard; Corwin,
  2026-11-03: "needs to have the text in the mini-map when we show info be
  LARGER: fill as much space as that gives"; "Why no images? This is supposed
  to be the baseball or flashcard or scoreboard or boxscore highlights, or
  several of these paginated").  The card's words are as large as fit in the
  map, found by measuring, from 1.10's sizes up to four times them, and fitted
  again when the map changes size.  Its art is large: the shape's own
  picture, or for several matches their pictures side by side, or a picture
  the page gives from its own origin (art: { src, alt }, an <img> with alt
  text; never another host).  Its facts are a box score, label over value in
  cells, when there is room, else lines; facts that do not fit are left out,
  not cut.  Several matches are pages, one card each after the first, at most
  9 (the house number 9: a person turns them one at a time, and "9 of 9" stays
  one digit); TopoForge.cardPage() turns them, and a new message,
  topoforge:cardpage, tells the page.  On a phone-wide screen, where the map
  is hidden, it shows while a card does, along the drawing's foot.
- The pad that pages the cards (switch cardpad; it turns bigcard and mappad
  on): while a card shows the pad turns 45 degrees and takes the accent
  colour, so it is clear something happened.  Its upper arms go back (left the
  previous card, up the first), its lower arms forward (right the next, down
  the last); every cell's tooltip and name say so, and the turn is said.  When
  the card goes, the pad is its 1.11 self again.

Version 1.11 (2026-11-02; checked: tests/run.sh, the project-files check, the
browser kit and the embedding kit green, with their planted faults; see
RESULT.txt and RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release packed or signed
yet)

- The map's pad, for a page that asks (switch mappad; Corwin, 2026-11-02
  17:27: "bring the D-pad gadget"; "left/right/center: mini-map mode"; "a
  movable control for positioning/calibration").  A cross that hangs on the
  overview map's edge and moves with it.  Its horizontal bar is a drawn
  slider for the map's mode: Preview shapes on the left, Both in the middle
  (the default), Highlight matches on the right; the thumb glides between
  them.  Its vertical arm sets the map's scale (Closer, Farther; a press on
  the thumb is the whole drawing), and its grip moves the map.
  TopoForge.padArrows('pan') turns the arms into panning the drawing
  instead.  A new message, topoforge:mapscale.
- Reset layout (switch reset; TopoForge.resetLayout() and undoReset()):
  every panel and the overview map (place, size, scale, mode) back where
  they started, the drawing untouched; Undo reset puts them back.  In the
  map's Mode menu and the View menu; the page gets topoforge:reset.

Version 1.10 (2026-11-02; checked: tests/run.sh, the project-files check, the
browser kit and the embedding kit green, with their planted faults; see
RESULT.txt and RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release packed or signed
yet)

- The overview map's modes, for a page that shows a preview (Corwin,
  2026-11-02 12:48): TopoForge.mapMode('cards' | 'both' | 'rings'), both by
  default.  Rings is 1.9's look: matches ringed in the map; its caption now
  goes to the page (or stays in the map with { mapCaption: true }).  Cards
  shows a card about what the page points at (a picture, a title, facts and
  one sentence), the map fading behind it.  Both shows the card and lights
  the matches in the drawing itself.  The map keeps its place and size
  through every mode.  The page may give the card's words with
  preview(what, { card }); the app fills in what it leaves out.
- TopoForge.mapScale(n): the map's own zoom, 1 (all of the drawing) to 8.
  With the map's handles shown, a Mode menu offers the modes and the scale.
- A new switch, mapwheel: the wheel over the map zooms the drawing,
  Alt+wheel the map's own scale, Shift+wheel moves the view east and west,
  Alt+Shift+wheel north and south.
- New messages, to a parent page of the same origin only: topoforge:preview
  (the caption and card of each preview) and topoforge:mapmode.

Version 1.9 (2026-11-02; built in stages on the corwin branch; checked: tests/run.sh,
the project-files check, the browser kit and the embedding kit green, with their
planted faults; see RESULT.txt and RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release
packed or signed yet)

- For a page that embeds the app (corwin.cloud's requests 1 to 7, blessed by
  Corwin on 2026-11-02).  New calls: TopoForge.view and .setView({ k, x, y });
  .selectionRect(), the selection's box on screen (also as rect in the select
  message); .place(null), to give up an armed shape; .setProps(id, patch), to
  change a shape's fields as one undo step.  New switches, each off until a page
  asks, and none of them turned on by ?embed=all or bare:
  - view: a message when the zoom or the place of the drawing changes;
  - keys: Alt+x, F10, C-x and C-c pressed in the drawing go to the page (with
    the keys after C-x or C-c, until the page says done, or C-g or Escape).
    While it is on, the drawing's own Ctrl+X and Ctrl+C are the page's; Cut
    and Copy stay in the Edit menu;
  - context: a message on a right click, a long press or the menu key on a
    shape (the app's own menu still opens);
  - pagelist: a message when a page is added, renamed, moved or removed.
  All of these reach a parent page of the app's own origin only.
- The overview map, for a page that asks (switch map): moved and resized by
  handles that sit outside its frame and appear as the pointer comes near
  (always on a touch screen, and whenever one has the keyboard focus); the
  grip and the resize handle take the arrow keys, and Position is a menu
  for one pointer.  Its place is kept in this browser.
- A preview in the overview map (switch preview): TopoForge.preview(ids,
  refs, a category or a kind) rings those shapes in the map, dims the rest
  and writes a short caption; preview(null) clears it.
- Page tabs that do not fit, everywhere: the strip shows the tabs that fit,
  with < and > to move along them, and All, a list of every page with a
  filter.  No more scrollbar in the strip.  Arrows, Home and End move among
  the page tabs (they used to nudge the selected shapes).  When the tabs
  fit, nothing looks different.

Version 1.8 (2026-11-01; built and checked: tests/run.sh, the project-files
check, the browser kit and the new embedding kit green; see RESULT.txt and
RESULT-embed.txt in tests/browser/; no release packed or signed yet)

- The name: topology forge (formerly TopoForge), made by Bryant Frankford,
  with Corwin Brust and contributors.  Its work now goes on in topology,
  branch corwin.  Our own text says the new name; what saved drawings and
  embedding pages rely on keeps the old one (window.TopoForge, the TopoForge
  JSON format, the topoforge.* storage keys), and so do the brand and the
  title in the app, which a visitor opening it on its own still sees.  The
  About box says the new name and Bryant first.
- Opened on its own, the app looks and works exactly as 1.7 did (the
  embedding kit compares the two, pixel for pixel, at three widths, in both
  themes).  A page that embeds it may now ask, with ?embed=... or
  TopoForge.embed({...}), for:
  - frameless: no brand, no document name, no rules between the areas;
  - props and shapes: Properties and Shapes float over the drawing,
    starting at its right and its left.  Each has a grip row: drag it, or
    use the arrow keys on it, or its Move menu (no dragging needed), and
    Hide or Show.  Places are kept in this browser.  Narrower than 861
    pixels nothing floats;
  - pages: the page tabs, a Pages list and the status line join the
    overview map in one floating box, and the bottom bar goes;
  - select: the app tells the page what is selected (name, tooltip, kind,
    page) and when the page changes;
  - ready: the app tells the page when the drawing named by ?load= is open;
  - hide-menus, hide-tools, hide-shapes, hide-props, hide-pages: each piece
    of the app's own chrome hidden separately (bare hides them all), so
    the page can draw its own;
  The last two reach a parent page of the app's own origin only, never any
  other.  spec/EMBED-v1.md is the whole of it.
- New calls for an embedding page: TopoForge.version, .selection, .ready
  (a promise), .showPage(name or index), .panels(), and .commands() with
  .run(id): every command in the menus, by id, label, shortcut and whether
  it is enabled, so a page that hides the app's chrome can offer them all
  from its own prompt; .shapes(), the palette's catalogue with its
  pictures; and .place(id), to place a shape as the palette would (in the
  middle, at a point, or armed: the next press on the drawing drops it).
- Found, not changed: the green "done" message that appears for a few
  seconds after a drawing is opened is white on green at 3.3 to 1, under
  the 4.5 to 1 that WCAG AA asks.  It is as it was in 1.7, so a visitor
  opening the app on its own sees the same thing; changing its colour is
  for Bryant and Corwin to choose.

Version 1.7 (2026-10-27; built and checked: tests/run.sh and the project-files
check green, the browser kit 41 of 41 in Chromium 153 with axe-core 4.14 and no
axe finding of any level; no release packed or signed yet)

- A page on the same origin can open a drawing with ?load=<path> (a CSV or
  TopoForge JSON); any other origin, and file://, is refused and said;
  window.TopoForge.importCSV for the kit.

Version 1.6 (2026-10-08; built and tested; no release packed or signed yet)

- The drawing tells its own story: a Story panel beside Properties, Layers
  and Issues says in plain sentences what the drawing shows (devices, which
  group holds which, what connects to what) and, apart from that, what the
  drawing does not say (no address, no subnet, a loose end, a shape drawn over
  a group but not in it).  It updates as you draw.
- Export the story as Markdown, meant to be given to a person or an AI
  together with the picture.  It carries the facts it was built from, so
  anyone can check it against the drawing.
- Lasting handles: each shape and connection gets a handle in its data
  (tf_ref) when it is drawn; handles survive export and import, where the
  internal ids do not.
- The project files a free-software package carries: README.org, AUTHORS,
  COPYING, COPYING.LESSER, LICENSES/, REUSE.toml, NEWS, ChangeLog, INSTALL,
  and RECIPE.org, from which the scripts in bin/ are made.
  Licences: code GPL-3.0-or-later OR LGPL-3.0-or-later; prose CC-BY-SA-4.0.
- Accessibility fixes, including some in what came from 1.5:
  - The menu buttons (File, Edit, ...) are plain buttons that say they
    open a menu and whether it is open; Escape closes a menu and puts you
    back on its button.  (1.5 called the row a "menubar" but its items were
    ordinary buttons, which misled screen readers.)
  - Every field in the Properties panel is tied to the words beside it,
    so a screen reader says "IP address", not just "edit text".  The X, Y, W,
    H boxes and the custom-data name and value boxes are named the same way;
    custom data now shows "Field name" and "Value" above its boxes.
  - The faint grey text (headings in the panels, hints, the status bar) is
    darker in the light theme and lighter in the dark theme, so it is
    readable (at least 4.5 to 1 against its background); the story's
    "text alternative" note uses the stronger grey.
  - Headings no longer skip a level: the page title is level one, the
    panel headings level two, the story's sections level three.  They
    look as before.
- How we know: tests/run.sh (the story checked against the drawing by a
  second program, with planted faults that must be caught) is green, and
  the browser kit tests/browser/topoforge.browser.mjs, run in Chromium 141
  with axe-core 4.14, passes all 34 of its checks in light and dark themes, with no
  axe finding of any level,
  (tests/browser/RESULT.txt, which the kit writes itself).  Not yet tried: a
  real screen reader, browsers on Windows, and drawing a shape with the
  keyboard alone.

Version 1.5 (Bryant Frankford, 2026-10-08)

The version received from Bryant (sha256
6a76e9ca66e6a2733a35677c11599114b4009529568580526d90427fa08a9e2a).  What it
does, read from the file itself:
- Devices (33 kinds, each with a role from outside world to endpoint), groups
  (zone, site, VLAN, subnet, DMZ, VPC, rack; nestable), basic shapes,
  connections glued to a side or free, pages and layers.
- Network fields on devices (hostname, IP, MAC, VLAN, status, vendor, model,
  subnet, notes) and connections (speed, medium, interfaces, notes), plus
  free custom data.
- Issues panel: bad and duplicate IPs, an IP outside its subnet, VLAN range
  and mismatch, duplicate hostnames and interface names, loose ends,
  self-loops.
- Save and open TopoForge JSON; export SVG, PNG, PDF, CSV (and a CSV
  template), draw.io, Visio .vdx (2003-2010) and .vsdx; import JSON, CSV,
  draw.io and Visio.
- Autosave in the browser; undo and redo; keyboard shortcuts for the menus.
- Note: its About box still says "Version 1.0".

